Z||000004||T-top Fiero||07-26-2001||07:44 PM||||The valve cover is available either from GM (part# 10031327)or Edelbrock (part#4257). ||||reg||1
Z||000005||b52buf||07-26-2001||08:57 PM||||Thanks Mr. T-Top your help is greatly appreciated. Made my search a lot shorter.<BR>Thankx again,<BR>Larry ||||reg||14
Z||000006||Dave Gunsul||07-26-2001||09:19 PM||||Hey T-top that looks really good!!<BR>What mods did it take to get it to fit?<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||14
Z||000007||T-top Fiero||07-26-2001||10:01 PM||||Here is a list of modifications that had to be performed in order for it to fit a stock 84-86 2.5 setup:<BR>1. Cut down stock air filter housing to an open element style housing.<BR>2. Relocate temperature gauge sending unit from top of head to thermostat housing.<BR>3. Remove EGR valve.<BR>4. Remove decklid springs. A Rodney Dickman strut is used now. <BR>5. Remove engine lifting lug from front of head.<BR>6. Modify PCV valve to fit on side of new valve cover.<BR>7. Trim stud part of head bolt that holds brake booster vacuum line.<P>I know there are some differences between the 84-86 2.5 and the 87-88 2.5. I can not say for sure if it will fit on the latter.<P>Here is a shot from the other side so you can see the PCV valve location.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/SD3_3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
